  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Miyabenol A may interact with HepG2 through multiple pathways, with Ataxin-2 and 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14 being identified as targets. These targets are both involved in the regulation of cellular processes, and their interaction with Miyabenol A is supported by a consistent score of 0.55, indicating a moderate level of confidence in the relationship. The presence of a common target, (1R,3R,4S,7S,9S,11S,13R,14R,15R)-4,11,13,14-tetrahydroxy-7-[(2Z,4R)-4-(hydroxymethyl)hex-2-en-2-yl]-3,9,15-trimethoxy-12,12-dimethyl-6,17-dioxabicyclo[11.3.1]heptadecan-5-one, further supports the notion that Miyabenol A may act through a shared mechanism. The evidence is consistent across different pathways, suggesting that Miyabenol A may exert its effects by modulating the activity of these targets, potentially influencing cellular processes in HepG2 cells. The relationship is supported by multiple lines of evidence, but the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated."
